Item 09 is complete. Nothing remains to request: the report section, tables, data, tests, PROGRESS.md and iterations.md are all written, the full suite passed after the last code edit, and `solutions/` is untouched.

**Outcome.** No known threshold of the random bipartite incidence graph coincides with the hardness ridge. The deliverable is that statement with the numbers, plus the mechanism the ridge is actually a transition of.

**What was built.** `learning/cover_excess.py` models the customer–product incidence graph as a two-type configuration model with each generator's degree distributions and the empty-row repair. It derives core emergence thresholds and core cyclomatic numbers, peels real instances to check them, scores every candidate against §25's 25 measured peaks, and counts feasible and reachable closed sets exactly at n ≤ 25. Tests: `tests/test_cover_excess.py`, 12 cases on hand-checkable instances.

**Findings, written to `reports/ml_nature.md` §36:**
- The giant component sits 0.34 to 0.78 decades below the ridge, further below the fewer the products. The (3,2)- and (2,3)-cores appear 0.11 to 0.45 below with the same drift. The (3,3)-core crosses the ridge, so its small mean error is misleading. Connectivity moves with n while the ridge does not.
- Excess 2 at its natural value is the only line within 0.09 decades of every peak with a flat residual across ratios. Its tree-free forms, the 2-core's excess and the dominance-reduced excess, score the same when calibrated. They are one condition on the cover's cycle mass, not separable at the grid's resolution.
- The mechanism is reachability, not feasibility. The count of closed sets whose boundary fits `optimum − 1` is monotone in density and anticorrelated with node counts. The count of sets reachable from the empty set through fitting steps matches the default search's node count to 0.03 to 0.06 decades on connected instances and peaks where the nodes peak in all eight series at n = 20 and 25. That is why an incidence-graph threshold alone cannot locate the ridge.

**Files.** Analytic and empirical tables in `reports/cover_excess_tables.md`. Data under `learning/data/ensemble/` as two CSVs and three logs. The random-graph papers cited from memory are listed as wanted in `literature/MISSING.md`, none held.

**Verification.** Full suite after the last edit: 1,190 passed, 2 skipped, 1 xfailed in 343 s on a loaded machine.

**Next item.** Item 10 asks whether the ridge's height is a function of the same quantity. The per-instance core and reduced-instance sizes in `cover_excess_measures.csv` are available if the height fit wants a core size rather than n.

